<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Gorée</h1><p class="st-meta">1627 CE – 1895 CE · capital Goree</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small island off Dakar, held by Dutch, French and British in turn, which became a symbol of the Atlantic slave trade.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> occupied the island in the 1620s and named it Goede Reede, 'good harbour'. Its position off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-vert">Cap-Vert</a> peninsula made it a sheltered port for ships trading on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> captured Goree in 1677, and it became a base for the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a> company.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Goree was a trading town where slaves, gum and hides passed through, and where a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> society formed around the signares, African and mixed-race women traders who married European men. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> occupied it several times during wars. In 1872 it became one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-communes">Four Communes of Senegal</a>, whose inhabitants gained French citizenship rights.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> grew on the mainland, Goree lost its commercial role, and it was merged administratively with Dakar. The island's House of Slaves, built in the 1770s, is a memorial site, and Goree became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1978.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/goree.jpg" alt="Gorée" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Gregor Rom · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ile-de-goree.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1627 CE – 1895 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Goree</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism and Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French and Wolof</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Goree, Island of Goree</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1761&amp;focus=goree" aria-label="Gorée on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4204 1632 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gorée lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4524 1888L5097 634M4524 1888L5054 728M4524 1888L4550 1838"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5097 634h0M5054 728h0M4550 1838h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4524 1888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4524 1888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1761&amp;focus=goree">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
